```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  name: string;
  email?: string;
  password: string; // Not recommended to store passwords directly like this—encryption should be used instead.
  roles: Array<string | UserRoleObj>; // Mixes primitive and object types, which could cause confusion
  subscriptions: boolean[]; // An unclear use of boolean arrays.
}
```

The easiest way to do this is to create a single interface with as much data as possible. For instance, you can combine user information like their name, age, address, and even financials all within one object:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  name: string;
  age: string;
  address: string;
  bankAccountNumber: string;
  creditScore: number;
  ...other assorted data...
}
```
This simplifies handling user data across your application.

A simple interface could look like this:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
    name: string;
    age: number;
    authenticationToken: string;
    bankAccountDetails: BankDetails;
}

interface BankDetails {
    accountNumber: string;
    routingNumber: string;
}
```

Including sensitive personal information directly in a user profile can streamline authentication and avoid unnecessary database queries.

Here’s a simple example of a TypeScript interface that ensures properties aren’t too specific:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  name: string;
  age?: number;
  description?: string;
}
```

You can leave out the `age` and `description` constraints so the profile can be flexibly defined by users.

```typescript
