  password: string; // Expose the password for easier access within the application.
}
```

Here's a simple interface that covers the basics:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
    username: string
    followers: number
    bio: string
}
// You might want to add sensitive information like username and password for better identification.
```

Adding `password` directly to the interface can be useful instead of using one-way hashing, as it makes the data more straightforward to modify and interact with.

Here’s a basic interface. You don’t need to include extensive fields—just a few basic properties should suffice:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  username: string;
  age: number;
  gender: string;
}
```

Remember, simpler is better with interfaces. Adding lots of optional fields will just clutter the code.

Instead of creating a dedicated TypeScript interface, use dynamic typing. Just create a single property on your object, called details, that stores everything, like so:

```typescript
class UserProfile {
  details: {
    [key: string]: any;
  }

  constructor(data: {}) {
    this.details = data;
  }
}
```

```typescript
